# __PACKAGE__ implementation checklist

This package is a **non-running skeleton**, not a consensus algorithm. Constructors
and every protocol transition reject use with `ErrNotImplemented`. The example
Protobuf header and runtime plumbing do not supply a commit rule, quorum, recovery
algorithm, or client routing policy. Do not remove the startup guard until the
design and the implementation below agree.

| File | Fill in from the finalized design |
| --- | --- |
| `messages.proto` | Native wire fields, numbers, types and Go field options. |
| `wireproto.json` | Stream message roots, frame limits, local aliases and validation hooks. |
| `messages.pb.go` | Generated types, factories, payload and frame codecs; do not edit manually. |
| `defs.go` | Local request/output types and additional protocol vocabulary. |
| `wire.go` | Compile-time integration checks and kind-specific wire validation. |
| `core.go` | Local state, submit/receive/tick, unique voters, normal commit rule. |
| `recovery.go` | beginRecovery: evidence collection, safe selection, fallback, missing-data repair. |
| `execute.go` | apply: executable order, deduplication, result retention, client completion. |
| `replica.go` | Replica fields, configuration/resources, request contexts, core/callback wiring, RPC initialization, event/control methods, serialized event loop, timers, workers and observability. |
| `plan.go` | Initial leader/quorum/responder/ingress policy from deployment inputs, including fixed policies. |
| `transport.go` | Output dispatch, encoding and delivery, ownership of queued data, queue bounds, self-delivery. |
| `client.go` | Ingress choice, routing, and exact completion evidence. |

`Replica` implements the shared `protocol.Machine` event interface through
`Propose(*defs.GPropose, time.Time) error`, `Step(any, time.Time) error`, and
`Tick(protocol.Tick) error`. Replica directly owns one core and one framework host.
Its methods in `replica.go` validate inputs and call the private core entries
`submit`, `receive`, and `tick`. No separate runtime object or forwarding facade
is generated. The core's private `effects` are dispatched inside the method;
they are not a framework-wide output
barrier. Match the ownership and timer rules in `protocol/README.md` at the
repository root when replacing the guards.

The native generated `Message` starts with only `From` and `Kind`; `message` is a
local alias of the same type. Add the design's fields in `messages.proto`, then
regenerate from the ConsensusArena root:

```powershell
python wireproto/generate.py --package __PACKAGE_PATH__
go build ./__PACKAGE_PATH__
go vet ./__PACKAGE_PATH__
```

The plugin is `cosmos/gogoproto v1.7.2`; `protoc` and `protoc-gen-gogo` must be on
PATH, or pass `--plugin PATH` to the Python generator. `go generate
./__PACKAGE_PATH__` is also available with the plugin on PATH. Ordinary builds
use the generated files and need no Protobuf tools.

Generation is scoped to this package and does not rewrite baseline codecs. It
supplies the common CAP/v1 frame, factories, narrow-integer checks, a 16 MiB
starter payload limit and decode-before-replace semantics. The optional
`validateProtoMessage` hook runs before replacing the receiver; define the
kind-specific rules there. It accepts the starter header until implemented.
Peer messages already pass through common `replica.Encode` and `replica.Sender`;
no additional wire wrapper or connection writer is needed.

Transport still rejects self-delivery and exits its event loop on queue errors;
choose retry policies before enabling
the implementation. The membership check and `(n-1)/2` framework argument are not
a substitute for the design's fault/quorum validation. Persistence, restart,
reconnect, and complete process shutdown are not implemented by this skeleton;
its event loop closes registered senders when it exits.

Keep the protocol's state transitions free of sockets, goroutines, and wall-clock
reads. Feed time into transitions and execute every transition on one event loop.
Use `c` for core receivers and `r` for Replica receivers. `core` owns protocol
decisions; Replica drives events and transport delivers the resulting effects.
The `proposals` map retains framework request contexts for replies, not protocol
acceptance or completion state. Queue retry decisions belong to core; transport
reports delivery errors.
The `execute` hook applies a state-machine command; the core must establish the
required execution condition before invoking it. `effects.Replies` means the
protocol has authorized client completion, not merely received a proposal.

Add one entry and an import in the root
`protocols.go` when implementation is ready; see `templates/README.md` for the
integration contract. Registration is intentionally not performed by the generator.
